# Universe content · nebulas and gas clouds
# researched: PBS Star Gazers "The 5 Types of Nebulae"; WorldAtlas "The Different Types of Nebulae";
#   astrophoton.com nebula guide; plus standard astronomy for star-forming structures
# Spec: docs/CONTENT-FORMAT.md · wiki/systems/r05-universe.md

# ── The five main kinds ──

@nebula emission
name: Emission nebula (H II region)
real: yes
color: mostly red
says: gas lit up from inside by hot young stars; where new stars are being born
example: the Orion Nebula

@nebula reflection
name: Reflection nebula
real: yes
color: blue
says: dust that only reflects the light of nearby stars
example: NGC 1999

@nebula dark
name: Dark nebula
real: yes
color: black against the stars
says: dust so thick it blocks the light behind it
example: the Horsehead Nebula, the Coalsack

@nebula planetary
name: Planetary nebula
real: yes
color: blue-green and red
says: the glowing shell a dying star of about one solar mass throws off; nothing to do with planets
example: the Helix Nebula

@nebula supernova-remnant
name: Supernova remnant
real: yes
says: the wreck of an exploded star, a shock wave sweeping up gas as it spreads
example: the Crab Nebula, from a star seen exploding in 1054

# ── Star-forming structures ──

@cloud molecular-cloud
name: Molecular cloud
real: yes
says: a huge, cold, dark cloud of gas and dust, the raw material for new stars

@cloud giant-molecular-cloud
name: Giant molecular cloud
real: yes
says: a molecular cloud big enough to build thousands of stars

@cloud bok-globule
name: Bok globule
real: yes
says: a small, dense, dark knot of dust where one or a few stars may form

@cloud pillar
name: Pillar of creation
real: yes
says: a tall column of dust and gas carved by the light of nearby hot stars, with new stars forming inside

@cloud herbig-haro
name: Herbig-Haro object
real: yes
says: bright knots where jets from a newborn star crash into the gas around it

@cloud protoplanetary-nebula
name: Protoplanetary nebula
real: yes
says: a short-lived cloud around a dying giant, just before it becomes a planetary nebula

@cloud wind-bubble
name: Stellar wind bubble
real: yes
says: a hollow blown in the gas by a strong star's wind

# ── Generator rules ──

@rule nebula-seed
real: rule
says: every nebula is generated live from the world seed; nothing is pre-built

@rule nebula-light
real: rule
says: a cloud glows red if hot stars light it from inside, blue if it reflects starlight, and black if there's no light behind it

@rule nebula-birth
real: rule
says: molecular clouds and their globules and pillars collapse into new stars and solar systems over time

@rule nebula-death
real: rule
says: dying stars of about one solar mass leave planetary nebulas; exploding heavy stars leave supernova remnants that seed new clouds with heavy elements

@rule nebula-place
real: rule
says: star-forming nebulas sit mostly in a galaxy's spiral arms

@rule nebula-travel
real: rule
says: thick nebulas block sight and slow travel; they are also rich in gas resources

# ── the gas's light, from the elements ──
# nebula-gas: every element in a glowing nebula by its cosmic abundance (content/elements.unt abundance), each nebula its own
#   scatter about it (scatter-dex) and its galaxy's metals; a planetary nebula's own by the measured averages of 58 of them
#   (Kingsburgh & Barlow 1994, as tabled by Kwitter & Henry 2022: helium, carbon and nitrogen dredged up by the dying star).
#   Each element glows in its own lines (lines: recombination, as hydrogen's and helium's; nebular-lines: the forbidden lines
#   that cool the gas, made brighter per atom by forbidden-boost, measured against Orion's [O III] 5007 at about three times
#   H-beta), each line its own colour by its wavelength, so the gas is the sum of every element's real spectrum. How hard it
#   is lit (excitation, from 0 for a cool gas to 1 for a planetary nebula's hottest) sets which ions there are: neutral and
#   once-ionised atoms at its edges, twice-ionised and more round the hot star in its middle. Its density (log electrons per
#   cubic cm) puts out the forbidden lines whose levels are emptied by collisions first ([S II], [O II] in a dense gas). Every
#   ion of every element lies in its own knots as well as the cloud's shared ones, so no two parts lie the same.
@law nebula-gas
name: The gas's light
forbidden-boost: 2700
scatter-dex: 0.15
emission-density: 2..4
emission-excitation: 0.1..0.65
planetary-density: 3..4.5
planetary-excitation: 0.35..1
remnant-density: 2..3.3
remnant-excitation: 0.15..0.55
planetary-abundance: He 11.05, C 8.60, N 8.24, O 8.66, Ne 8.04, S 6.87, Ar 6.27
# dust-av: how much the nebula's own dust dims its light, in magnitudes at visual (0 none; a few in a dusty cloud), blue
#   dimmed more than red (the dust's extinction falls as about one over the wavelength), so the same gas reads orange, gold,
#   brown or rust behind its dust; starlight-share: how much of its light is its star's own, scattered by its dust, in its
#   star's colour (star-k, the lighting star's heat in kelvin: blue-white to orange); excitation-spread: how far a nebula's
#   lighting strays past its kind's range (some lit so hard the twice-ionised oxygen's green-teal rules, some so softly the
#   ionised nitrogen's and sulfur's deep red does); glow: how bright the gas is drawn against the stars, at any distance
#   (the far look, kept up close); colour-share: how much of each line's own pure colour is kept against its brightness
#   (the rest its brightness in grey: a glowing gas, never a neon tube)
dust-av: 0..3
starlight-share: 0..0.35
star-k: 3500..40000
excitation-spread: 0.25
glow: 0.35
colour-share: 0.7
